; CONVERTING A LIMB TABLE TO THE HOST'S OWN BIGNUM, so that it can be printed.
;
; A SEPARATE FILE because it names `big+`, `big*` and `big-of` — the language's
; arbitrary precision, injected only on a target that HAS one. windows declares
; none, and loading this there fails to cover, which is why the portable core in
; `bignum.oro` must not mention them: that host can compute in limbs and cannot
; render the result, and those are two different capabilities.



; ------------------------------------------------------------- to the host
;
; A limb table converted back to the host's own bignum, so that it can be
; printed. No host prints a limb table, and every program that computes a big
; value has to say it eventually.
;
; Horner from the top would be shorter; this walks upward with a running power
; so that the loop reads each limb once and the analysis sees a counted loop.
;
; It names `big+`, `big*` and `big-of`, which are the LANGUAGE's arbitrary
; precision — injected on every target that has one (targets/*/bigint.oro). A
; target without one cannot use this and the lowering refuses there by name,
; which is the same answer JavaScript already gives for the checked primitive it
; does not declare.
(def to-host (fn (n lim a)
  (loop ((acc (big-of 0)) (p (big-of 1)) (i 0))
    (>= i n)  acc
    else      (again (big+ acc (big* p (big-of (at a i))))
                     (big* p (big-of 16777216))
                     (+ i 1)))))
